+# Decision: How committed changes reach browsers
+
+## Context
+
+Every board has one authoritative sequence of event revisions, produced by editors' HTTPS commands. Staff browsers — up to 800 at once across the markets — show a view of that board and need each committed change soon after it happens: a stall closure or a power problem that sits unseen is the failure MarketBoard exists to remove.
+
+How changes travel from the server to those browsers shapes three components at once. The change store's read and publication paths, the delivery component's whole reason for existing, and the browser's subscription and recovery logic all take a different form under each delivery route. Choosing the route late, or reversing it, means redesigning all three.
+
+Several things hold whichever route is chosen, so they do not separate the options. Every route can show committed changes within the 15-second freshness goal. Every route delivers the same authoritative revisions. Every route has to bring a browser back to the latest board after up to 20 minutes disconnected. And updates carry only stall IDs and operational statuses, so nothing about the data rules out sending them through a third party.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option; all three are viable. The choice is made on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Update latency — major.** How long a committed change takes to appear in a connected browser. The goal is 15 seconds, but coordinators act on what the board shows, and at two changes a minute with opening-time bursts, a board that trails by seconds is one people double-check in the group chat it was meant to replace.
+- **Independence from a realtime provider — major.** Whether delivery carries a recurring third-party fee and whether delivery and browser code are written against one provider's subscription model. No tenet sets this weight — managed infrastructure is permitted — so it is a judgment about this path specifically: delivery is the part of MarketBoard every browser touches continuously, and the part most expensive to rewrite.
+- **Operational and build burden — moderate.** What MarketBoard must build, run, and be on call for: connection capacity, behavior during deploys and restarts, and the machinery for catching a browser up. This bears directly on the 99.5 percent availability goal, since everything self-operated is something that can be down during event hours.
+- **Visibility of connectivity failures — moderate.** How naturally the route lets a browser tell "nothing has changed" from "I have stopped hearing about changes". Its importance comes from the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board.*
+- **Load proportional to change — minor.** Whether server work tracks the number of changes or the number of browsers. A busy market averages two changes a minute; most moments on most boards have nothing new.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Each browser asks the server every five seconds for revisions after the last one it has seen. The server answers from a cursor query over the event's revisions and holds no per-browser state. Delivery is ordinary request/response behind the same HTTPS infrastructure that already serves commands; a browser's "subscription" is a timer.
+
+A browser returning after twenty minutes offline does exactly what it does every five seconds — asks for what it missed — so catch-up is not a separate mechanism.
+
+- Update latency: **partial**. A change appears up to five seconds after commit, plus request time; inside the goal, never immediate.
+- Independence from a realtime provider: **strong**. No provider is involved.
+- Operational and build burden: **strong**. Stateless servers, no long-lived connections, no reconnect logic; deploys and restarts are invisible to browsers beyond one failed request.
+- Visibility of connectivity failures: **strong**. A poll either returns or fails; a browser knows within one interval that it has lost contact.
+- Load proportional to change: **weak**. 800 browsers produce about 160 requests a second regardless of activity, nearly all returning nothing.
+
+Summary: the least to build and run, paid for with a few seconds on every update and constant read load.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+Each browser holds a WebSocket to servers MarketBoard operates, for the event it is viewing. When a revision commits, the change store publishes it to delivery, which pushes it down every socket connected to that event. A browser that drops reconnects and asks for the revisions after the last one it saw, which the change store replays.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. A change is pushed as soon as it is published, typically well under a second.
+- Independence from a realtime provider: **strong**. The protocol is a web standard and the servers are MarketBoard's own; there is no fee beyond the hosting it already pays for.
+- Operational and build burden: **weak**. MarketBoard owns capacity for 800 long-lived connections, the wave of reconnects when a server restarts or is deployed, reliable publication from the store to delivery, replay, and browser reconnect and catch-up code — none of which exists under polling.
+- Visibility of connectivity failures: **partial**. A cleanly closed socket is obvious, but a connection can die silently and look identical to a quiet board. Detecting that requires heartbeats and a check for gaps in revision numbers, which must be designed in.
+- Load proportional to change: **strong**. Servers do work when a revision commits, not on a timer per browser.
+
+Summary: immediate updates on infrastructure MarketBoard controls, paid for by owning connections and recovery end to end.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A third-party service holds the browser connections. When a revision commits, MarketBoard publishes it to the provider, which fans it out to subscribed browsers through the provider's client library. Delivery becomes a thin wrapper around the provider's publish API and its subscription credentials. Because a publish to the provider can fail or be missed by an offline browser, the change store reconciles what was committed against what was published, and browsers still fetch missed revisions from MarketBoard after a long disconnection.
+
+- Update latency: **strong**. Comparable to self-hosted sockets.
+- Independence from a realtime provider: **weak**. A recurring fee that grows with connections and messages, and delivery and browser code written to one provider's channels, credentials, and client library.
+- Operational and build burden: **partial**. Connection capacity and reconnects are the provider's problem, and this is the fastest route to low-latency updates. What remains is publication reconciliation, credential issuing, and a dependency whose outages MarketBoard cannot fix during event hours.
+- Visibility of connectivity failures: **partial**. Provider libraries report connection state, but whether a browser has missed a revision is still only knowable against MarketBoard's own revision numbers.
+- Load proportional to change: **strong**. MarketBoard publishes once per revision.
+
+Summary: low latency with the least connection work, paid for in fees and in coupling to one vendor.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+It is the only option strong on both major criteria. Polling keeps MarketBoard independent but gives up immediacy on every single update; the hosted service gives immediacy but puts a vendor's pricing and client model on the one path every browser uses all day. Self-hosted sockets get both, and are also the option whose load follows activity rather than headcount.
+
+The cost is real and accepted knowingly: this is the weakest option on operational and build burden. MarketBoard takes on connection capacity and reconnect behavior as its own work, in exchange for fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor. Polling would have been the right answer had minimum operational burden been the overriding concern; it is not.
+
+On the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible*, this option is only partial where polling is strong, so the tenet becomes an obligation rather than a property that comes for free.
+
+Committing to this obligates:
+
+- **Change store** — revisions replayable from any revision a browser last saw, across at least the 20-minute recovery window, and publication to delivery that does not lose a committed revision.
+- **Delivery** — capacity for 800 simultaneous sockets and 20-edit-per-second bursts, and defined behavior when a server restarts and its browsers reconnect together.
+- **Browser sync** — reconnect, catch-up from the last seen revision, detection of silently dead connections and of gaps in revision numbers, and a visible disconnected state whenever the browser cannot vouch for being current.
+
+Because catch-up reads revisions over a request to the change store, the store's replay path is close to what polling would have needed. If operating the socket tier ever puts the 99.5 percent availability goal at risk, falling back to polling against that path is the cheaper retreat; moving to a hosted service would mean rewriting delivery and the browser subscription.
