 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets deliver committed changes.** Browsers hold a socket to servers MarketBoard operates, and committed revisions are pushed down it. Two alternatives also meet the 15-second freshness goal. Five-second HTTPS polling is the simplest to operate — stateless request/response over cursor queries — but leaves a few seconds of lag on every change and repeats reads from every browser whether or not anything changed. A hosted realtime service gives low-latency updates with the least connection operations work, but adds recurring fees and couples delivery and browser subscription code to one provider. Self-hosting takes on connection capacity and reconnect handling as MarketBoard's own operational work in exchange for fast updates with no realtime vendor dependency. The criteria and each option's full case are in `docs/decisions/change-delivery.md`.
